      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blend the fish, garlic, {{fish-sauce}}, {{cornflour}}, egg, dried chilli flakes, and {{caster-sugar}} in a food processor until smooth and paste-like."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fold in the shredded kaffir lime leaves by hand. Refrigerate the mixture for 30 minutes to firm up."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Shape the mixture into 12 small round patties about 1.5 cm thick using damp hands."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a wide frying pan over medium-high heat. Fry the patties in batches for 3 minutes per side until golden and cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain on kitchen paper. Serve hot with lime wedges and a sweet chilli dipping sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Patties falling apart in the pan",
              "cause": "Mix was not chilled or fish was not blended finely enough",
              "fix": "Return mix to the fridge for a further 15 minutes. Add an extra teaspoon of cornflour if still soft."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Inside is raw while outside is dark",
              "cause": "Pan was too hot",
              "fix": "Reduce heat to medium. Thinner patties cook more evenly. Aim for 1.5 cm thickness."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Cakes taste bland",
              "cause": "Under-seasoned fish mix",
              "fix": "Fry a small test patty before shaping all of them. Adjust fish sauce or dried chilli in the remaining mix."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thai fish cakes are sold from market stalls and street food carts across the country. They are always served with a sweet chilli dipping sauce and sometimes cucumber relish. The bouncy, chewy texture is what sets them apart from fish cakes in other traditions. This comes from blending the fish to a paste rather than leaving it chunky. Kaffir lime leaves are the defining flavour. The dish travels well and stays good for an hour at room temperature, which is part of why it works so well as a market snack."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
